STATELESS VS STATEFUL

Stateful (Barista): You walk in and say, "The usual, please." The barista remembers you from
yesterday and knows exactly what you want.
The "state" (your preference) is stored in the barista's head.

Stateless (Vending Machine): The machine has no idea who you are. Every time you want a soda,
 you must provide the money and press the button. It doesn't care if you bought ten sodas a minute ago;
  each transaction is brand new.

How it Works in Software:

In a stateless architecture, the server does not store any information about the user's previous requests.
Every single request sent from the client (your Java app or browser) must contain all the information needed
to identify the user and complete the task.

The Stateful Way (Traditional)

Client: "Login me in. I'm Adam."

Server: "Okay, I'll save 'Adam is logged in' in my memory (Session)."

Client: "Show me my profile."

Server: "Checking my memory... ah yes, you are Adam. Here is your profile."

The Stateless Way (Modern/JWT)

Client: "Login me in. I'm Adam."

Server: "Here is a signed JWT that says you are Adam. I'm going to forget we ever spoke."

Client: "Show me my profile. (Attaches JWT)"

Server: "I don't know you, but this JWT is valid and says you are Adam. Here is your profile."

Why Stateless is Better for Scaling

This is the "magic" reason why statelessness is so popular in modern Java development: Horizontal Scaling.

If you have a massive app with 5 different servers, a stateless request can go to any of them.

In a Stateful app: If you log in on Server A, but your next request goes to Server B, Server B will
say "I don't know who you are!" because your session is stuck in Server A's memory.

In a Stateless app: Since the "ID card" (the JWT) is in the request itself, Server B can validate it
just as easily as Server A. It doesn't need to "remember" anything.